Z||000009||Monkeyman||10-01-2000||08:21 AM||||'68 and '69 SS Chevelles had the 396s.  The others had the small block (can't remember if it was the 305 or the 350).  My ex-brother-in-law had a '69 SS Chevelle.  Seems like there was another Chevelle with a big block, but I can't remember for sure.  Maybe the '7? Chevelle "Heavy Chevy"?||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||10-01-2000||08:57 AM||||SS stands for Super Sport. The Super Sport was the Performance package, but not all SS's were big blocks, in fact most were small blocks. For instance in '69 you could get a Camaro SS with a 350, a 396, or a 427. The SS packages also usually included a nice hood scoop, rumbly exhaust, and some other minor appearance items.<P>RS stands for Rally Sport. Rally Sport was the appearance package. The RS's could be had with a 6cyl or V8, but the V8 was not part of the RS package, it had to be ordered as a separate option. Again on the 69 Camaro example, the RS had fancier tail lights, hideaway headlamps, chrome louvers on the hood, and other minor appearance items.<P>Then there was the RS/SS - the best of both worlds, The Z-28 built to conform to racing, and the ever-so-rare ZL1 with the aluminum 427!<BR> <BR>Personally I'd stick with Pontiac badges on a Fiero, such as the WS6 or ram-air. I would probably laugh at a Fiero SS unless it had a Chevy V8 in it. There are only a few ocasions that justify non-oem badges on a car.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000011||turbo86se||10-01-2000||12:04 PM||||On a similar note, this past summer I went to the Williamsburg Fiero show.  I pulled up to the hotel under the canopy and a guy from the club came outside and told me where to register. He thought I was a poser because I have custom sail panels that say "TURBO".  They're really sweet. Of course he changed his mind when I opened the rear decklid.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||My7Fieros||10-01-2000||02:02 PM||||Ive never really been too big into Camaro, or Chevy's, so Im not too familiar w/ the SS and RS series (I was into Trans Ams before I got into Fieros). Judging from what everyone says, it kinda sounds to me like the Fiero Gt is their version of the SS, while the Se is their version of the RS...does that make any sense? I personally wouldnt go w/ the SS or RS badging...some die hard Camaro/Chevy enthusiasts might fly off the handle on that one. What I would do however, is if you want other badgings on your car, stick w/ the Pontiac vehicle badging..such as Ram Air or WS6...I take off the Fiero badge on the front of mine when I repaint them and fill in the holes..not a big fan of them.<BR>I have to admit though, I do like the GT emblem that comes off the new Grand Prix's...(or is it Grand Ams?)..sweet!||||reg||1
