Z||000023||mrfixit58||10-04-2000||03:17 PM||||Rockcrawl,<P>Good responce on the SS/RS thing. One exception... you said: "Then there was the RS/SS - the best of both worlds, The Z-28 built to conform to racing, and the ever-so-rare ZL1 with the aluminum 427!" Actually, the Z-28 came with a 302. It was designed to compete is a particular class of SCCA racing. <P>I believe the 427 your referring to could be ordered but is most commonly known as the motor-of-choice as built by Yanko. And it was mainly used in straight line drag racing. I saw one that Reggie Jackson bought and restored at an auction. The bidding stopped at $93,000 and didn't sell.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Tigger||10-04-2000||03:32 PM||||It's Yenko, not flaming just letting you know.<P>Yenko I'm pretty sure was a Chevrolet dealer in Grand Rapids MI.  Basically the "Yenko" was a new factory Camaro Z28, Nova, Vega, and maybe a Corvair upgraded with a performance package.  Mainly a BIG HP engine!  Best thing, the stickers and stripes on the car actually meant something!<P>Funny quotes from Yenko owners:<P>"Hey, let go of my Yenko"<P>"Here, pull my Yenko"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000025||wahoo4u||10-04-2000||04:09 PM||||not to get away from the sport car badging, but i am picking up a set of red and black 2.8FI tags for my 86 SE  those badges came on the cavaliers with 2.8, the celebrities came with crome 2.8 tags.  This way you can let everyone know what they just got beat by when you fly past them.  Although i like the idea of a fiero ss  racing stripes and a v8 are a must though.||||reg||3
Z||000026||rockcrawl||10-04-2000||04:13 PM||||mrfixit58,<BR> Maybe I should have worded that a little better. The regular production option (RPO) Z/28 was built to conform to racing and had a 302 ci engine. The Z/28s are a dime a dozen compared to the ZL1. The ZL1 (COPO 9560)came with a 427 with aluminum block and heads, There were only 69 of them built by the factory. They fetch a pretty penny, the last one I heard of sold for $300,000. COPO 9561 had a cast iron 427. All Yenko Camaros (in '69) started out as a COPO 9561 with factory 427/425hp engine. They also had COPO 9737 Sport Car Conversion option. They also received the yenko stripe and emblems as well as any options the customer ordered. Before 1969, the 427s were swapped into the Camaros by Yenko, but in 69 Yenko worked with the factory to create the COPO 9561 option. The same car could then have been ordered by any dealer, the only thing that makes it a Yenko is the stripes that were added at the dealership. The original Yenko dealership was in PA, but they did sell cars nationally.<P>In case you couldn't tell, I am a 69 Camaro nut. My first car was a 69 Camaro 327. Of course that was before I knew about Fieros!!!<P>Sorry for the off-topic chat.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
